Microservices Architecture

1. What is Microservices Architecture?
	A microservices architecture structures an application as a collection of small, loosely coupled, independently deployable services.
	Each service:
		Has a specific responsibility
		Can be developed independently
		Can be deployed independently
		Communicates through APIs
		Can potentially use its own technology stack
	                Application
		            │
	       ┌────────────┼────────────┐
	       ↓            ↓            ↓
	   User Service  Order Service  Payment Service
	       │            │            │
	      DB           DB           DB

2. Why Microservices?
	Independent Deployment
		You can deploy one service without redeploying the entire application.
		Order Service → deploy v2
				   ↓
		User Service → unaffected
		Payment Service → unaffected
	Independent Scaling
		If one service receives heavy traffic, scale only that service.
		User Service      → 2 instances
		Order Service     → 10 instances
		Payment Service   → 3 instances
	Fault Isolation
		Failure in one service doesn't necessarily bring down the entire application.
	Technology Freedom
		Different services can use technologies suited to their specific requirements.

3. How to Decompose Services
	The article recommends decomposing around business capabilities.
	For an e-commerce system:
		Product Catalog
		Inventory
		Orders
		Delivery
		Users
		Recommendations
		Reviews
	Each capability can become a service.
	important principle - Design services around business capabilities, not technical layers.
	
4. Don't Share Databases Directly
	Bad
		Service A ──→ Database A
				 ↑
				 │
		Service B ───────┘
		Service B directly accesses Service A's database.
	Problem - DB Schema changes -> Service B breaks -> Service A can't change independtly
	Better - Service B -> Service A API -> Database A
	The service owns its data and exposes only the required API.
	This preserves encapsulation and independent evolution.
	
5. Design APIs Carefully
	A service should expose only what clients actually need.
	Service
	 ├── Internal implementation ❌
	 ├── Internal DB structure  ❌
	 └── Public API             ✓
	Avoid exposing implementation details because clients may start depending on them.
	Also choose communication protocols carefully. A tightly coupled protocol can make future changes difficult.
	
6. Decentralize Ownership
	A team should ideally own its service end-to-end:
		Team
		 │
		 ├── Develop
		 ├── Test
		 ├── Deploy
		 ├── Monitor
		 └── Maintain
	This reduces dependencies on separate support teams.
	you can use a message bus such as RabbitMQ, but don't too much business logic into a central bus.
	
7. Deployment
	Consumer-Driven Contracts
	If Service B depends on Service A:
		Service B
		   ↓
		expects API contract
		   ↓
		Service A
	A consumer-driven contract records what the consumer expects from the provider.
	Before changing Service A:
		Change API
		    ↓
		Run consumer contracts
		    ↓
		All pass?
		    ↓
		Deploy
	This helps prevent one service's API changes from unexpectedly breaking another.
	
8. Containers
	Multiple services on one OS
			Server
			 ├── Service A
			 ├── Service B
			 └── Service C
		Problems:
			Dependency conflicts
			Harder independent scaling
			Services can affect each other
	Isolated Services - 
		Container A → Service A
		Container B → Service B
		Container C → Service C

9. Changing APIs in Production
	Approach 1: API Versioning
		/api/v1/orders
		/api/v2/orders
		Keep both versions temporarily.
		consumer migrate - v1 → v2 Then remove v1. Problem: maintaining multiple versions becomes expensive.
	Approach 2: New Endpoint
		Instead of maintaining two complete API versions:
			/api/orders
			/api/orders/new-operation
		Migrate consumers to the new endpoint and eventually remove the old one.
		The article highlights this as easier to maintain because only one service version remains active.
		
10. Standardization
	With many independent teams, define common standards for:
		API design
		Error handling
		Documentation
		Testing
		Metadata
	Tools such as Swagger/OpenAPI can help document and test APIs.

11. Service Discovery + API Gateway
	As services increases:
		Order
		 ↓
		Payment
		 ↓
		Inventory
		 ↓
		User
		 ↓
		Shipping
	Finding service instances becomes difficult because hosts and ports can change dynamically.
	
	Service Discovery
		Answers: "Where is the Payment Service currently running?"
	API Gateway
		Provides a common entry point:
			                 ┌── User Service
			Client → Gateway ├── Order Service
					 ├── Payment Service
					 └── Inventory Service
		It can also handle things like authentication/authorization.
		
12. Failure Handling
	Microservices are not automatically resilient.
	Failures can come from:
		Network timeouts
		Bugs
		Dependency failures
		Service crashes
	A failure in one service shouldn't cascade through the entire system.
	
	Bulkhead 
		Isolate resources so one failing component doesn't consume everything.
			Service A → Pool A
			Service B → Pool B
			Service C → Pool C
		If A fails:	
			Pool A ❌
			Pool B ✓
			Pool C ✓
	
	Circuit Breaker
		Failures
		   ↓
		Threshold exceeded
		   ↓
		Circuit OPEN
		   ↓
		Stop calling failing service
		   ↓
		Wait
		   ↓
		Test recovery
		   ↓
		CLOSED
		
	This prevents repeated calls from overwhelming an unhealthy dependency.
	
13. Monitoring and Logging
	Microservices create a major observability problem: 100 services * 10 instances * lots of logs, Good luck finding one request manually. Humanity has invented better ways.
	
	Centralized Logging
		Service A ─┐
		Service B ─┼──→ Log Aggregator → Search/Dashboard
		Service C ─┘
		
		Logstash -> Elasticsearch -> Kibana
	
	Metrics
		Aggregate things such as:
			CPU
			Memory
			Request rates
			Errors
			Latency
	Health Checks
		Services should expose health endpoints so monitoring systems/load balancers can determine whether instances are healthy.
		
Microservices architecture divides an application into independently deployable services organized around business capabilities, with each service owning its responsibilities and exposing APIs while using mechanisms such as service discovery, API gateways, contracts, fault isolation, and centralized observability to operate reliably at scale.

